### Comprehensive List of SA Health Infection Prevention and Control Reporting Requirements

#### **1. Infection Prevention and Control and Healthcare Associated Infection (HAI) Surveillance and Reporting Policy**
- **Mandatory Reporting**: Health services must report healthcare-associated infections (HAIs) and comply with the National Safety and Quality Health Service (NSQHS) Standards [1](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/about+us/governance/policy+governance/policies/infection+prevention+and+control+and+healthcare+associated+infection+%28hai%29+surveillance+and+reporting+policy).
- **Surveillance**: Regular surveillance of HAIs to identify trends and implement strategies to minimize infections [1](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/about+us/governance/policy+governance/policies/infection+prevention+and+control+and+healthcare+associated+infection+%28hai%29+surveillance+and+reporting+policy).

#### **2. Prevention and Management of Infection in Healthcare Settings**
- **Standard Precautions**: Implementing standard precautions for all patients, including hand hygiene, use of personal protective equipment (PPE), and aseptic techniques [2](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.).
- **Transmission-Based Precautions**: Additional precautions for patients with specific infections, such as isolation and use of specialized PPE [2](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.).

#### **3. Healthcare Associated Infection Surveillance**
- **Data Collection**: Collecting data on the incidence of HAIs to monitor and improve infection control practices [3](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/clinical+resources/clinical+programs+and+practice+guidelines/infection+and+injury+management/healthcare+associated+infections/healthcare+associated+infection+surveillance/healthcare+associated+infection+surveillance).
- **Reporting**: Submitting regular reports on HAI data to relevant authorities to ensure compliance and continuous improvement [3](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/clinical+resources/clinical+programs+and+practice+guidelines/infection+and+injury+management/healthcare+associated+infections/healthcare+associated+infection+surveillance/healthcare+associated+infection+surveillance).

#### **4. Notifiable Disease Reporting**
- **Public Health Act 2011 (SA)**: Reporting notifiable diseases to public health authorities as required by law [1](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/about+us/governance/policy+governance/policies/infection+prevention+and+control+and+healthcare+associated+infection+%28hai%29+surveillance+and+reporting+policy).

#### **5. Antimicrobial Stewardship**
- **Policy Compliance**: Adhering to the SA Health Antimicrobial Stewardship Policy to ensure appropriate use of antimicrobials and reduce resistance [1](https://www.sahealth.sa.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/public+content/sa+health+internet/about+us/governance/policy+governance/policies/infection+prevention+and+control+and+healthcare+associated+infection+%28hai%29+surveillance+and+reporting+policy).


Healthcare-Associated Infections (HAIs) are primarily considered **patient incidents** because they occur in patients while receiving medical treatment in healthcare settings such as hospitals, outpatient clinics, and nursing homes [1](https://www.cdc.gov/healthcare-associated-infections/php/data/progress-report.html). However, HAIs can also have public health implications, especially if they lead to outbreaks or involve antibiotic-resistant organisms [2](https://www.cdc.gov/healthcare-associated-infections/index.html).

